## Why This Is Asked
Event-sourced systems impose strong invariants across replicas; this tests that replay, ordering, and failure handling preserve correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Event sourcing invariants
- Idempotency and deduplication
- Replay reliability and snapshots
- Out-of-order handling and shard rebalancing
- Fault injection and chaos testing
- Mutation testing on event handlers

## Code Example
```javascript
function testReplay(events, apply, invariant) {
  let state = {};
  for (const e of events) {
    state = apply(state, e);
  }
  if (!invariant(state)) throw new Error('Invariant failed');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generate mutation tests for event handlers?
- What production metrics would you surface to detect latent replay bugs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes testing of a real-time, multi-tenant streaming system with shared infrastructure (Kafka, MongoDB). It tests end-to-end latency, exactly-once processing, tenant isolation, privacy-focused test data, and canary-based deployment practices.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency (p95/p99)
- Exactly-once semantics and deduping
- Tenant isolation on shared resources
- Data privacy and redaction in tests
- Canary deployments and rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dedup logic in consumer
let seen = new Set();
while ((ev = nextEvent()) !== null) {
  if (seen.has(ev.id)) continue;
  seen.add(ev.id);
  process(ev);
}
```

